WHY AGPL AND NOT MIT

MIT lets anyone take this work, close it, and sell it, with no obligation to
anyone. That is not what is intended.

AGPL-3.0 is OSI-approved and genuinely open source: you may read, run, modify
and redistribute this software. What it adds is section 13 — if you run a
MODIFIED version of this software as a network service, you must offer your
users the complete corresponding source of your version, under this same
licence.

For a hosted web application that is the clause that matters. Someone may fork
this and compete; they may not fork it, close it, and compete.

WHY THE SOURCE IS PUBLIC AT ALL

Because the product makes claims that are worthless unless they can be checked:

  - that traveller, guest and fan money sits in escrow and always reaches a
    terminal state — released to the host, venue or artist, or refunded — with
    the rules enforced by the contracts rather than by us
    (contracts/src/Bookings.sol, Venues.sol, Tickets.sol, TripVault.sol)
  - that a trip vault can only pay vendors inside the caps its owner set, and
    only inside the trip window (contracts/src/TripVault.sol)
  - that the wallet key is derived in the browser from the traveller's passkey
    and is never stored or sent anywhere (ui/src/auth/)
  - that there is no hidden gas sponsorship: every user pays their own network
    fee, quoted before anything is signed (ui/src/auth/wallet.js, ui/src/gas.js)
  - that $TOURS are loyalty points, not an investment, and are non-transferable
    in points mode (contracts/src/ToursToken.sol)

Those are checkable here, by reading the code. That is the point of publishing
it.

